<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>

<section xml:id="sec_activecode_decision_tree_classifier" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>ActiveCode Exercise: Decision Tree Classifier for Obstacle Detection</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>This exercise uses a lightweight decision tree classifier, such as scikit-learn's <c>DecisionTreeClassifier</c>, to sort labeled data coordinates into "obstacle" or "free space" categories. Students train the model on a small labeled dataset of <m>(x, y)</m> coordinates and then use it to classify new, unlabeled coordinates.</p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec_decision_tree_dataset">
    <title>Building the Training Set</title>
    <p>Each training example is a coordinate pair labeled "obstacle" or "free space," based on whether that location was occupied in a reference map. Students assemble a list of features (the coordinates) and a matching list of labels, then fit the classifier with <c>model.fit(features, labels)</c>.</p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec_decision_tree_activecode">
    <title>Try It</title>
    <p>The activecode environment does not depend on an external machine-learning library being installed, so this exercise builds a minimal decision-tree classifier from scratch: it searches over candidate thresholds on the <m>x</m> coordinate and keeps whichever threshold best separates the training labels. The <c>fit</c>/<c>predict</c> interface below mirrors how a library like scikit-learn is used.</p>
    <program xml:id="ac_decision_tree_classifier" interactive="activecode" language="python">
      <input>
class SimpleDecisionTree:
    def __init__(self):
        self.threshold = None

    def fit(self, features, labels):
        candidate_thresholds = sorted(set(x for x, y in features))
        best_threshold = None
        best_correct = -1
        for t in candidate_thresholds:
            correct = 0
            for (x, y), label in zip(features, labels):
                predicted = "obstacle" if x &gt; t else "free space"
                if predicted == label:
                    correct += 1
            if correct &gt; best_correct:
                best_correct = correct
                best_threshold = t
        self.threshold = best_threshold

    def predict(self, new_coordinates):
        return ["obstacle" if x &gt; self.threshold else "free space" for x, y in new_coordinates]


features = [(3, 1), (4, 3), (5, 2), (6, 1), (7, 4), (9, 2)]
labels = ["free space", "free space", "free space", "obstacle", "obstacle", "obstacle"]

model = SimpleDecisionTree()
model.fit(features, labels)
print(f"Learned split threshold: x &gt; {model.threshold}")

new_coordinates = [(7, 2), (2, 2)]
predictions = model.predict(new_coordinates)
for coord, label in zip(new_coordinates, predictions):
    print(f"{coord} -&gt; {label}")
</input>
    </program>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec_decision_tree_prediction">
    <title>Classifying New Coordinates</title>
    <p>After training, <c>model.predict(new_coordinates)</c> returns a predicted label for each new coordinate. Students compare predictions against a held-out set of coordinates with known labels to estimate how well the classifier generalizes beyond its training data.</p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec_decision_tree_test_case">
    <title>Test Case Interpretation</title>
    <p>Given a training set where all coordinates with <m>x &gt; 5</m> are labeled "obstacle" and all others are labeled "free space," a correctly trained decision tree should predict "obstacle" for a new coordinate such as <m>(7, 2)</m> and "free space" for <m>(2, 2)</m>. If the classifier misses this simple pattern, students should check that features and labels were paired correctly before training.</p>
  </subsection>
</section>
